"Affinities": {
  "index": -94,
  "category": "sorcery",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "title",
      "value": "Affinities"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "json": [
        {
          "text": "I've noticed that as wizards perform magic, they tend to align themselves with the elements of the spells they are casting.  There are six main ones, called "
        },
        {
          "text": "Affinities",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": ".  They are:\n\n"
        },
        {
          "text": "Air",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": ", which opposes "
        },
        {
          "text": "Earth",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": "\n"
        },
        {
          "text": "Water",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": ", which opposes "
        },
        {
          "text": "Fire",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": "\n"
        },
        {
          "text": "Ender",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": ", which opposes "
        },
        {
          "text": "Arcane",
          "color": "blue"
        },
        {
          "text": "\n\nEvery affinity seems to have an opposite, as I've noted above.  Wizards with higher affinities in a given element seems to be able to "
        },
        {
          "text": "cast their spells of the same element with greater ease",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": ", and seem to be able to "
        },
        {
          "text": "memorize parts of the spell that share their affinity faster",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": ".  However, they appear to have a "
        },
        {
          "text": "similar level of difficulty with elements that oppose their affinity",
          "color": "dark_purple"
        },
        {
          "text": ".\n\nBalance in all things, I suppose."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}